  "shorts": [
    {
      "short_id": "short268",
      "angle": "Two nursing-home agreements sent every dispute to arbitration except claims for the home's own late payments.",
      "avoids": "No claim that the clauses were held valid; no merged families; no named Supreme Court author or guessed vote; no invented relationship.",
      "funnel_question_left_for_longform": "Can a family member's signature bind the patient who never signed? The Supreme Court left that question open.",
      "loop": true,
      "loop_join": "The last line returns to the single exception carved into the opening clause.",
      "era": "West Virginia, 2009-2012",
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "L1",
          "text": "Two nursing-home admission agreements required arbitration for every dispute but one exception: claims to collect late payments owed by the patient.",
          "delivery": "intense",
          "source_lines": [
            "No carve-out for late payments. Nothing about who pays to file."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L2",
          "text": "The paper therefore sent negligence and death claims to a private arbitrator while preserving a courthouse path for collecting an unpaid bill.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "A dispute about how somebody died goes to a private arbitrator. A dispute about an unpaid bill stays in a courthouse."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L3",
          "text": "A family member had signed each agreement on behalf of a patient who required extensive nursing care.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "In each case, a family member of a patient requiring extensive nursing care had signed an agreement with a nursing home on behalf of the patient."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L4",
          "text": "The Supreme Court's short opinion provides no ages, conditions, admission dates, death dates, or details of the alleged care.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "No ages. No conditions. No dates of admission, no dates of death."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L5",
          "text": "The same two contracts also placed the arbitration filing fee on whichever party filed first, without stating a dollar figure.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Whoever starts the proceeding pays to start it. The opinion states no dollar figure."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L6",
          "text": "Marchio's agreement was materially different: it required arbitration too, but had no late-payment exception and said nothing about filing fees.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Marchio's paper was not that paper."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L7",
          "text": "That difference is why the three families cannot be merged into one story or one supposedly identical form.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Three families. Two documents."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L8",
          "text": "For Brown and Taylor, the asymmetry is simply what the paper says: every dispute goes private, except the home's route to collect late payments.",
          "delivery": "calm",
          "source_lines": [
            "The relevant parts of the agreements in Brown's case and Taylor's case were identical."
          ],
          "claims": []
        }
      ],
